    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Weather Report</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Today's Weather Will Be Sunny with Some Rain Later.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: communication: Confirm comprehension of peer contributions during discussions. concept: Use diagrams to explain concepts to peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can confirm understanding of peer contributions by writing keywords and showing visuals (e.g., write 'bring materials' and point to a list of supplies). Can use diagrams to explain weather patterns to peers (e.g., draw a sun and clouds to show sunny and rainy weather). Can summarize weather-related concepts for classmates by combining words and diagrams (e.g., write 'water cycle' next to a simple diagram).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various weather flashcards or simple drawings (sun, cloud, rain, wind, storm) on the board. Students quickly say the weather word. The teacher then shows a simple diagram of a weather prediction (e.g., a sun and a cloud for "sunny with some clouds") and asks, "What will the weather be like today?" prompting students to use the future simple tense. The teacher also displays simple diagrams of actions (e.g., a person with an umbrella) and asks, "What should you bring?" linking to modal verbs for recommendations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to explain a weather concept using both words and a drawing. For example, the teacher says, "It will be sunny tomorrow," while drawing a sun on the board. Then, the teacher models confirming understanding: "Do you understand 'sunny'?" and points to the drawing. The teacher also models segmenting the word 'thermostat' into syllables: "ther-mo-stat" and asks students to repeat.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If your friend says 'It will be windy' and you don't know 'windy,' what can you draw to help them understand?" (Expected answer: "Wind lines.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I draw a sun and say 'sunny,' how do you show me you understand?" (Expected answer: "Say 'Sunny!' and point to the sun.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Weather Report".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple, illustrated weather forecast infographic for the local area for the next two days (e.g., a digital slide or printed poster). It includes icons for sun, clouds, rain, wind, temperature numbers, and uses future simple tense, prepositions of place, and modal verbs. For example, "Tuesday morning: It will be sunny. Temperature 28°C. Tuesday afternoon: Clouds will appear over the mountains. You should bring a jacket. Wednesday: Expect rain in the east."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describe weather conditions: 'It will be sunny tomorrow.' Ask about the forecast: 'What's the temperature tomorrow?' Use prepositions of place: 'The rain is in the east.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher presents the infographic. Students individually answer gist questions orally or by pointing to the infographic. Teacher emphasizes intonation in predictions.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What will the weather be like on Tuesday morning?" (Students point to the sun and say "Sunny!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where will the clouds appear on Tuesday afternoon?" (Students point to mountains and say "Over the mountains.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"True or False: It will be cold on Wednesday." (Students respond based on the temperature icon.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What should you bring on Tuesday afternoon?" (Students identify "jacket.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Future simple tense for weather predictions, prepositions of place (on, at, in, over), modal verbs for recommendations (should). Vocabulary: weather, forecast, report, cloud, storm, rain, wind, sun, temperature, predict, look like, bring, sunny, cloudy, warm, cold, rainy, windy, clear, dry, wet, hot.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Diagram Explanation Match (Controlled Practice):</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences describing weather conditions (e.g., "It will be sunny and warm," "A storm will be over the lake," "You should bring an umbrella for the rain.") and a set of simple weather diagrams (sun icon, sun with thermometer up, storm cloud over a lake, umbrella with rain). Students match each sentence to the corresponding diagram, drawing a line to connect them. This reinforces using diagrams to explain concepts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Peer Keywords & Visuals (Semi-controlled):</b> In pairs (Student A and Student B), Student A reads a short, simple weather prediction (e.g., "Tomorrow morning, it will be cloudy. The temperature will be cool. In the afternoon, the sun will appear, and it will be warm."). Student B's task is to confirm comprehension by writing down 3-4 keywords (e.g., cloudy, cool, sun, warm) and drawing quick, simple visuals (cloud, downward thermometer arrow, sun, upward thermometer arrow) as Student A speaks. Student A then checks if Student B's keywords and visuals accurately represent the forecast. Roles are then swapped. This directly addresses confirming comprehension using keywords and visuals.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Summarize with Words and Diagrams (Pair Interaction):</b> Students work in new pairs. Each pair receives a slightly more complex weather scenario on a card (e.g., "A strong wind will blow over the city in the evening. You should expect the temperature to decrease. It looks like a cold night."). Students discuss how to summarize this information for another pair. They prepare to explain it using 2-3 sentences, 2-3 keywords written on a small whiteboard or paper, and 1-2 simple diagrams. They practice explaining to each other, ensuring clarity and use of future tense and appropriate vocabulary. For example: "It will be windy (draw wind lines) in the evening. The temperature will decrease (draw down arrow). Bring a jacket."</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Weather Report".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group "Weather Report Mediation" of their Digital Weather Forecast Video plan.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in their project groups (from the 21st Century Skills Project: Digital Weather Forecast Video). Each group has already brainstormed ideas for their digital weather forecast video (e.g., who will present which part, what weather conditions they will describe).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Now, each group prepares to "mediate" (explain and clarify) a specific 30-second segment of their planned video to another group or to the teacher. They must clearly explain what will happen in that segment.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They must use:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Future simple tense for predictions (e.g., "Our video segment will show a sunny morning.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Prepositions of place (e.g., "The rain will be over the mountains.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Keywords written on a small whiteboard or paper to summarize.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Simple diagrams or gestures to explain concepts (e.g., drawing a sun and pointing up for "temperature will increase," or drawing a cloud for "cloudy").</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The receiving group or teacher listens and asks questions to confirm understanding (e.g., "So, it will be sunny *and* warm?" "Where will the storm be?"). The presenting group must use their mediation skills to clarify.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Example mediation:</b> "Our first segment will be about the morning. It will be very sunny (draws sun), and the temperature will increase (draws upward arrow for temperature). So, you should wear light clothes. Does that make sense?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> In pairs, students receive a new, short weather prediction (3-4 sentences) that they haven't seen before. Student A explains this prediction to Student B, focusing on mediation techniques: using future tense, appropriate vocabulary, keywords written down, and simple diagrams drawn on paper. Student B actively listens and asks clarifying questions, then confirms understanding. Both students use a peer assessment checklist to evaluate each other's mediation skills. The teacher circulates and observes, providing feedback.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation & Peer Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can explain weather conditions using future simple tense and relevant vocabulary (sunny, cloudy, rain, wind, temperature).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can use diagrams to clarify concepts for their peer (e.g., sun for 'sunny', cloud for 'cloudy').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can confirm understanding of their peer by asking simple questions (e.g., "Do you understand 'windy'?" or "What should we bring?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses prepositions of place correctly (e.g., 'over the lake,' 'in the east').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intonation in weather predictions is clear and audible.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on an exit ticket:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can I explain today's weather using a drawing and important words? (Yes/No, sometimes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy or difficult about explaining weather to my friend using pictures?</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What did I learn today about explaining weather to my classmates using words and pictures?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can using diagrams help me explain things better in our Digital Weather Forecast Video project?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific mediation strategies (e.g., drawing, keyword writing, rephrasing) worked well for the students in explaining weather concepts and confirming comprehension?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to effectively summarize weather-related concepts for classmates by combining words and diagrams? What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed for students who struggled with this?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How effectively did students integrate the future simple tense, prepositions of place, and modal verbs into their mediation tasks?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "Our Weather Report" theme by consolidating all learned linguistic competences through mediation for the Digital Weather Forecast Video project. The next sequence of lessons will involve the actual production and presentation of these digital videos, focusing on integrated skills in a larger project context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>